overview.assistanceListingDescription:
Kapapahuliau implements the intent of the following Administration Priorities:
1) EO 14414: Advancing Regenerative Agriculture and Strengthening American Farm Resilience;
2) EO 14308: Empowering Commonsense Wildfire Prevention and Response;
3) EO 14276: Restoring American Seafood Competitiveness;
4) EO 14225: Immediate Expansion of American Timber Production; and
5) Invasive Species Mitigation: supports efforts to protect the security of the United States from invasive species, consistent with the priorities articulated in E.O. 14239, 14308, and 14313 and the National Farm Security Action Plan.

In alignment with the above Administration initiatives and the overarching Program Goal and Guiding Principles, the Kapapahuliau Program has been designed with four (4) Priorities of focus.  Applicant projects will need to address at least one of the following:
1.	Wildfire mitigation and upland erosion control through planning, agriculture, and environmental restoration (e.g., fire breaks, fuel load reduction, soil stabilization, and revegetation – including for native species timber production and traditional agricultural practices);
2.	Flood and drought mitigation using traditional Native Hawaiian agricultural and aquacultural systems to decrease nearshore sedimentation and coastal flood impacts, improve groundwater recharge, increase water access, and enhance food availability;
3.	Tidal inundation and coastal erosion adaptation to protect infrastructure, Native Hawaiian burial sites, traditional aquacultural systems, and other wahi pana (historic and sacred places);
4.	Invasive species management to mitigate impacts on Native Hawaiian livelihood activities (e.g., traditional practices, economics, tourism, etc.), and ecosystem services (e.g., sedimentation, fresh water, food, and native biodiversity) while supporting habitat conservation.

assistanceUsage.description:
Potentially eligible education programs and facilities may include, but are not limited to:
•	Schools and college campuses;
•	Public libraries;
•	Vocational training;
•	Rehabilitation of mentally or physically handicapped facilities;
•	Alternative schools;
•	School administration and maintenance facilities; and
•	Athletic fields.

